<<speech "maera">>He is waiting. Caelan?<</speech>>

I pause at the corridor's turn.

<<speech "maera">>The binding is not shame. It is armour. Remember that.<</speech>>

I say nothing. I walk toward the Iron Garden with a strip of maid's linen binding my chest, and the warmth of her hands lingers on my ribs like a brand.

[[Enter the Iron Garden|warrior_queen_day_1_4]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_4

The Iron Garden lies wreathed in grey mist when I arrive. The dawn has not yet committed itself to the sky, the light pewter, uncertain, and the cobblestones slick with dew that will burn off within the hour.

Sir Aldric Vornhelm stands at the yard's centre like a boulder that has grown weary of the mountain and walked down to kill something.

He is everything a warrior is supposed to be. Broad shouldered. Thick armed. Scarred across the jaw and temple and forearms, each mark a record of violence survived. His grey hair is pulled back in a tight knot. Frostfang, that monstrous broadsword, five feet of castle forged steel, hangs at his hip as naturally as another man's hand hangs at his side. His arms are folded. His face betrays absolutely nothing.

He does not greet me. He does not acknowledge my arrival. He tosses me a longsword, the same blade I have trained with since the age of twelve, twenty-eight inches of familiar steel, and speaks.

<<speech "aldric">>Strike me.<</speech>>

I catch the blade, settle into the wide-legged power stance carved into my bones through a thousand drills, and lunge.

He disarms me in three strokes.

Not through superior strength, though he possesses that in terrible abundance. Through //geometry//. My thrust overextends by two inches. My shoulders, narrower now than they were even a moon ago, cannot generate the same rotational force behind the blade. The lunge that should drive the point toward his belly drifts left, pulled by a centre of gravity that has migrated //downward//, into hips that are wider, a pelvis quietly, stubbornly reshaping itself beneath skin and sinew.

My leading foot lands wrong. My weight pitches forward and Frostfang's flat slaps the blade from my grip with contemptuous ease, sending it ringing across the cobblestones like a bell tolling for a funeral.

I stare at my empty hands.

The palms are softer than they should be. The calluses, built over years of gripping steel and leather, are thinning, the ridges smoothing, the skin beneath them finer grained and pale. The fingers are longer, more tapered. The knuckles less pronounced. A woman's hands growing inside a swordsman's gloves.

Even my grip is betraying me.

[[Try again|warrior_queen_day_1_5]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_5

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

I retrieve the sword. Set the stance. Drive forward with everything the old body knows how to give.

Disarmed in four moves. A marginal improvement that tastes like ashes.

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

We drill for two hours in that grey dawn. Every combination I mastered since boyhood misfires.

The //overhead cleave//, my finest stroke, the one that split practice dummies from crown to groin, the blow that made Aldric nod in approval three years past, pulls savagely to the left. The new weight on my chest, small as it is, shifts during the downswing. The binding Maera wrapped compresses the tissue but cannot eliminate its mass. Two small mounds of flesh, each barely larger than an egg, and yet they move, shift, and drag the blade's arc off true by inches that might as well be miles.

The //pivot and slash//, a manoeuvre I once executed in my sleep, wobbles and dies on its feet. My hips are wider. The rotation that once spun on a narrow male pelvis now grinds against a broader axis, and the footwork, the footwork I drilled ten thousand times, cannot account for bones that are moving, spreading, becoming something the footwork was never designed for.

The //rising parry// fails because my wrists lack the snap they had a month ago, the tendons looser, the joint more flexible, the grip that needs to lock rigid instead //giving//, absorbing force in a way that is perhaps useful for something but is categorically useless for this.

I grow furious. Not at Aldric, at this treacherous flesh that will not obey.

At the soft swelling beneath the binding that bounces when I lunge and aches when I twist, despite Maera's careful wrapping. At the hair that falls into my eyes, longer now and finer, slipping free of the tie I knotted at my nape. At the way my thighs rub together at the top during footwork, a friction that never existed before the hips began to spread and the flesh there began to thicken.

At the way my voice cracks between registers when I curse, neither the prince's baritone nor whatever it is becoming.

<<speech "mc">>Damn this useless body!<</speech>>

The curse comes out wrong. The first syllable is the prince's voice, low and hard. The second is something higher, not feminine, not yet, but //displaced//, as though the vocal cords are a string being slowly retuned and the pitch is wandering between notes.

Aldric's eyebrows do not move. His expression does not change. But I see him hear it. I see the flicker of recognition in those grey eyes.

He has heard my voice crack. He has heard the prince's baritone stumble and the beginnings of something else peek through the gap.

I want to die.

[[Aldric calls a halt|warrior_queen_day_1_6]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_6

<<speech "aldric">>Enough.<</speech>>

He sits upon a weapons rack, arms folded, always folded, as though keeping his hands occupied will prevent them from doing something violent to the nearest inanimate object. He regards me with those storm grey eyes that have watched men die on six battlefields.

I stand panting, dripping sweat, the longsword hanging from a grip that trembles with exhaustion and shame. The binding around my chest is damp and has loosened during the drills. I can feel the small mounds of new flesh shifting beneath it, no longer fully compressed, pressing against the leather chest piece with renewed insistence.

A long silence. The kind Aldric wields like a weapon, he can outwait stone.

<<speech "aldric">>Your fighting style was built upon a frame that no longer exists.<</speech>>

He says it as though remarking upon the weather.

<<speech "aldric">>The shoulders that drove your cleave are narrowing. Half an inch since last month, perhaps more. Your reach has shortened with them. The hips that anchored your stance are widening, I can see it in the footwork, the way the rotation catches where once it flowed. The chest that braced your guard now carries weight it was never shaped to bear.<</speech>>

He pauses. Lets the silence do its work.

<<speech "aldric">>The hands are softer. The grip weaker by measurable degrees. The wrists more flexible, which will be useful eventually but is a liability now. Your centre of gravity has dropped by two inches, perhaps three, the hips and the... chest... are redistributing your mass. The thighs are thickening with new tissue and your voice  <</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>My voice is fine.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Your voice is changing. I heard it crack just now. The baritone is becoming unreliable. Within a fortnight it will be gone entirely, if the potions hold their pace.<</speech>>

He delivers this catalogue of my unravelling with the clinical detachment of an armourer listing damage to a suit of plate. No pity. No revulsion. No embarrassment. Just facts, laid out in a row like blades on a rack.

I grip the longsword so hard my knuckles ache. The softer knuckles. The thinner fingers.

<<speech "aldric">>If you continue to fight as a man in a body that is becoming a woman's, you shall die. Not in training. On a field. With a blade in your belly and surprise upon your face, because the stroke that should have saved you pulled left by an inch and the foot that should have held firm slipped on a hip that was wider than your muscle memory expected.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Then what would you have me do?<</speech>>

The words come through clenched teeth. I taste copper. I bit the inside of my cheek during one of the failed drills and did not notice until now.

Aldric rises from the weapons rack. He walks toward me, not fast, not slow, with the measured gait of a man who has never once been in a hurry because the world has learned to wait for him.

He stops an arm's length away. Close enough that I can see the individual scars, the silver stubble, the deep lines carved by decades of squinting into sun and rain and the faces of enemies.

<<speech "aldric">>Tomorrow, we begin anew. From nothing.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>From nothing? I have trained since I was twelve  <</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>The boy who trained since twelve is gone. His body is gone. His reach, his strength, his balance, his centre, gone. Every technique you have mastered was built for a structure that is being dismantled. I cannot rebuild upon a foundation that is sinking. So I shall clear the ground and lay a new one.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>A new foundation. For what?<</speech>>

His grey eyes hold mine. Something moves in them, not warmth, exactly, but a kind of ferocious, guarded respect. The respect of a man who would not lie to you, even when the truth is a blade.

<<speech "aldric">>For whatever you are becoming.<</speech>>

He turns and walks away. Frostfang does not even rattle at his hip. The man moves like the weapon is part of him, an extension of a body he has never once been forced to doubt.

I stand alone in the Iron Garden and watch his shadow shrink across the cobblestones.

[[Stay in the yard|warrior_queen_day_1_7]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_7

I do not leave.

The mist burns off and the sun climbs the eastern wall, and I stay. I swing the longsword at nothing, at shadows, at air, at the ghost of the swordsman I used to be, and feel every stroke drift and pull and fail as the body beneath the blade continues its quiet rebellion.

At one point I stop and stand still, breathing hard, and become aware of something I have been refusing to notice.

The binding has slipped.

During two hours of lunging and slashing and the violent torso rotation of a swordfight, Maera's careful wrapping has worked loose. The linen band has shifted downward, bunching at my ribs rather than covering the chest. Above it, freed from compression, the small swells of my budding breasts sit against my sternum, unbound, unsupported, pressed against the leather chest piece with nothing between them and the rough hide but a sweat dampened tunic.

I can feel them. Not as pain, though they ache, a low, pervasive throb that pulses in time with my heartbeat, but as //presence//. As weight. As two soft, warm, undeniable additions to a chest that was flat for twenty years of my life.

They move when I breathe. A subtle rise and fall, the tissue expanding and contracting with the ribcage beneath, but //delayed//, the flesh responding to the breath a half beat behind the bone, like a wave following the wind. When I inhale deeply, they press outward against the leather. When I exhale, they settle, and the settling itself is a sensation, a gentle shift of weight and warmth that the old chest never produced.

I raise my hand and, with a self-consciousness that feels absurd given that I am entirely alone, press my palm flat against my left chest.

The flesh yields beneath my hand. Soft. Warm. Dense with the particular quality of tissue that is growing rather than simply existing. Beneath the pad of new breast tissue, I can feel the pectoral muscle, still present, still defined, but overlaid now with something that has no business being there and yet is //there//, unmistakably, and getting more there by the day.

The nipple presses against my palm. Larger than it was. Raised. The areola around it wider, the skin there slightly darker, slightly puckered, and so sensitive that the pressure of my hand sends a shiver down my spine that has nothing to do with cold.

I snatch my hand away.

<<speech "mc">>Stop it,<</speech>>

I tell the breast.

<<speech "mc">>Stop... //being// there.<</speech>>

It does not stop being there. It continues being there with the calm persistence of something given a task and intent on seeing it through regardless of the host's opinions.

I turn to the polished training shield mounted on the yard wall, the one used for checking stance and form, and look at my reflection.

[[Face the reflection|warrior_queen_day_1_8]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_8

The shield is bronze, polished to a mirror's clarity, and it does not lie.

The figure in the reflection is between things.

The face is still mine, mostly. The jawline has softened, the hard angles blurring toward something less angular. The cheekbones sit higher, or perhaps the cheeks are thinner, the face losing its squareness, trending toward an oval that is not yet feminine but is no longer definitively male. The brow is smoother. The lips fuller by a shade. The eyes are the same, the Velmoria grey, my mother's eyes, but set now in a face that frames them differently. More prominently.

//Pretty// is not a word I am willing to use. Not yet.

The hair falls past my shoulders in waves that the old straight, coarse prince's hair never produced. Thalara's potions have changed even that, the texture finer, the colour richer, the strands catching the morning light with a sheen that belongs on a noble lady's head, not on a warrior standing in a training yard with a sword in hand.

Below the neck, the reflection grows more troubling.

The shoulders are narrower, visibly, measurably narrower than they were at the start of the potions. The collarbone is more prominent, a horizontal ridge that catches shadows. The old broad, powerful upper frame that filled armour and swung swords is //shrinking//, and in its place is something lighter, leaner, more delicate in structure if not yet in appearance.

Below the collarbone, there they are. Even through the leather chest piece, even with the binding bunched uselessly at my ribs, the small swells of the budding breasts create a visible disruption in the chest's silhouette. Two gentle curves, slight but undeniable, pushing the flat leather outward by a fraction of an inch on each side. Enough to see. Enough for anyone with eyes to look at my reflection and know that the chest beneath that armour is no longer a man's.

The waist is narrower. The hips wider. The trousers hug curves that should not be there. Between the hip and the thigh, the profile has changed, rounder, fuller, the straight line of the male body curving inward at the waist and outward at the hip in a shape becoming, unmistakably, an hourglass.

I stare at the reflection. The reflection stares back. Neither of us blinks.

<<speech "mc">>Who are you?<</speech>>

I ask, quietly.

The reflection offers no answer. Reflections seldom do. But the face in the bronze, that softening, changing, almost pretty face framed by long hair and set above a body growing breasts and hips and curves, looks back at me with my mother's eyes, and for a moment I cannot tell whether I am looking at the prince who is dying or the queen who is being born.

A sound behind me. I spin, hand going to the sword, old instincts still sharp even if the body serving them is not, and find Torin.

[[Torin appears|warrior_queen_day_1_9]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_9

He stands at the yard entrance in his black and silver armour, his grey eyes fixed on me with the quiet, volcanic intensity that is simply //Torin//, the way water is wet, the way stone is hard.

How long has he been watching?

<<speech "torin">>Your Highness.<</speech>>

